(verb
  :name "concatenate!" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "destructive"
  :signature "(concatenate! lst) -> list"
  :summary "Flatten a list of lists by relinking their cons cells, destroying the original structure."
  :explanation "concatenate! is the destructive version of concatenate. It takes a list of lists and returns a new single-level list formed by relinking the cons cells of its sublists. The original list structure is destroyed - the sublists are no longer accessible in their original form. This verb reuses the cons cells of its arguments, meaning the original binding must be considered destroyed. The return value is the only thing a caller may rely on. The failure mode occurs when a caller keeps using the variable it passed in because the call seemed to work, but reads a list that has been cut or overwritten under it. On an implementation that quietly returns a fresh list instead, this bug would be invisible, which is why it went uncaught. For any verb whose name ends in `!`: the `!` means the argument's cells are reused, the original binding must be considered destroyed, and the return value is the only thing a caller may rely on."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define x (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))\n(concatenate! x)\n(list x)" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define y (list (list 'a 'b) (list 'c 'd)))\n(eq? y (concatenate! y))" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define z (list (list 10) (list 20) (list 30)))\n(define result (concatenate! z))\n(list (eq? z result) (eq? (car result) (car (car z))) (eq? (cdr result) (cdr (car z))))" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The original list structure is destroyed - the sublists are no longer accessible in their original form." "The implementation may reuse cons cells from the input lists, so the original binding must be considered destroyed." "The return value is the only thing a caller may rely on." "On an implementation that quietly returns a fresh list instead, the destructive nature of this operation would be invisible.")
  :drawbacks ("Destroys the original list structure." "Reuses cons cells from the input lists." "Requires careful handling to avoid unintended side effects.")
  :usecases ("Flattening nested lists into a single-level list." "Combining multiple lists into one while preserving order." "Efficiently concatenating lists without copying elements.")
  :related ("concatenate" "append!" "append" "flatten" "list")
  :learn (:concept "Destructive operations and their implications." :prerequisites ("list" "car" "cdr") :progression "Learn about destructive operations and how they differ from non-destructive ones. Understand the importance of managing side effects in functional programming.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 §concatenate!; implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js:955 (surface completion 2026-08-03); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-03"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define x (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))\n(define result (concatenate! x))\n(list (eq? x result) (eq? (car result) (car (car x))) (eq? (cdr result) (cdr (car x))))"
     :math-program "(define (test-concatenate! lst)\n  (let ((result (concatenate! lst)))\n    (and (not (eq? lst result)) ; original destroyed\n         (equal? result (apply append lst))))) ; same value\n(test-concatenate! (list (list 1) (list 2)))"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-concatenate! lst)\n  (if (pair? lst)\n      (concatenate! lst)\n      (error 'concatenate! \"argument must be a list of lists\")))\n(safe-concatenate! (list (list 'a) (list 'b)))"
     :problem "The operator wants to flatten a nested list into a single-level list. The operator needs to know whether the operation is destructive and what happens to the original structure."
     :explanation "eng: The original list x is destroyed, and the result is a new list formed by relinking the cons cells. math: The result should not be eq? to the original and should be equal to the result of applying append. cs: The implementation should check that the argument is a list of lists before performing the operation."
     :meta "Destructive operations like concatenate! modify their arguments, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the caller relies on the original structure.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations modify their arguments, so the caller must be aware of this side effect."
     :math "The result of concatenate! should not be eq? to the original list but should be equal to the result of applying append."
     :cs "The implementation should include checks to ensure the argument is valid before performing the operation.")
)
